Example 21.2 As an example, see how the template is filled following the argument we saw for Example 21.1:

We prove that L is not context-free using the pumping lemma for context-free languages. Let n be the pumping-lemma contant. Consider the word z=\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{a}}^n\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}}^n\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{c}}^n. It is immediate to check that |z|\geqslant n and z\in L. Given an arbitrary decomposition of z as z=uvwxy, with |vwx|\leqslant n and |vx|\geqslant 1, consider i=0. We prove that uv^iwx^iy\notin L. Since |vwx|\leqslant n this word cannot mention \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{a}}s, \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}}s, and \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{c}}s at the same time. Since otherwise it must contain all copues of \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}}s and some \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{a}}s and \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}}s, that is it must have length at least n+2&gt;|vwx|. No matter what vwx is there is always at least one symbol \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{s}}\in \{\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{a}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{c}}\} not mentioned in vwx and hence modifying vwx into w will not change the \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{s}}^n part of z. But |vx|\geqslant 1, so going from vwx to w we removed at least one symbol different from s. Hence it is impossible that uwy has the form \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{a}}^k\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{b}}^k\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{c}}^k for some k, that is uv^0wx^0y=uwy\notin L.




