Definition 25.1 (Computable function on \Sigma^*) A partial function f\colon \Sigma^*\to\Sigma^* is computable if there exists a Turing machine M s.t. on every input w

if w\in \mathrm{dom}(f), M with input w terminates in the accepting state with f(w) on its tape;
if w\notin \mathrm{dom}(f), either M rejects or runs indefinitely.



Definition 25.2 (Computable function on \mathbb{N}^k) A (partial) function f : \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N} is computable if there exists a computable (partial) function g : \{\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}\}^* \to \{\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}\}^* such that, for every (n_1,\dots,n_k) \in \mathbb{N}^k:

if (n_1,\dots,n_k)\in \mathrm{dom}(f), then g(\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_1}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_2}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\cdots\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_k}) = \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{f(n_1,\dots,n_k)};
if (n_1,\dots,n_k)\notin\mathrm{dom}(f), then \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_1}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_2}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\cdots\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}}\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{n_k}\notin\mathrm{dom}(g).









Tip



The details of the encoding will matter for the “complexity part of the course” but not now, for example to encode a tuple of numbers (n_1,\dots,n_k) we could have used \Sigma=\{\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{0}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}},\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{\#}}\}, a binary encoding for numbers and \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{\#}} as a separator. Or we could have even used the alphabet \Sigma=\{\textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}\} and as encoding 
(n_1,\dots,n_k)\mapsto \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{1}}^{2^{n_1+1}3^{n_2+1}5^{n_3+1}\cdots p_k^{n_k+1}}\ ,
 where p_k is the kth prime number. So for example the encoding of (0,0) is \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{111111}}, while the encoding of (1,0) is \textcolor{darkred}{\mathtt{111111111111}}.
Any other ``reasonable’’ encoding would be fine, it will result in the same class of computable functions, because a Turing machine can convert between reasonable encodings.



